    <edge source="86082134-46ed-4548-a27f-2a5fed9c25ac" target="101a86f6-cba5-43af-ae57-a5d5f17d8c1d">
      <data key="d3">While the grace period allows temporary extension of coverage, the eventual cost of switching to a permanent policy hinges on its classification, making long-term financial planning dependent on timely decisions made during the grace window.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="86082134-46ed-4548-a27f-2a5fed9c25ac" target="178f1ff5-b810-4b49-ac2e-26311d446d64">
      <data key="d3">While the death benefit described in fact_sentence_1 pertains specifically to a term policy, its existence informs why one might consider switching to a permanent policy—a move whose associated premiums are determined by the class of permanent policy, as noted in fact_sentence_2.</data>
      <data key="d4">informs</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="86082134-46ed-4548-a27f-2a5fed9c25ac" target="65530dff-13a5-4bc9-975b-c4c8ec2db1db">
      <data key="d3">While the first fact sets a clear monthly due date ($45 paid on the 15th), the second fact suggests that converting to a permanent policy may result in different premium calculations, potentially changing when and how much is owed, thus affecting the applicability of the original due date rule.</data>
      <data key="d4">informs</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
